![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

If `inventory` is `null` or not an object (which can happen if the JSON file is malformed or contains a primitive value like `null`), accessing `inventory.schema_version` or `inventory.rows` will throw a `TypeError` and crash the validator. Add a robust check to ensure `inventory` is a valid object before accessing its properties.

```suggestion
  const errors = [];
  const warnings = [];
  const isValid = inventory && typeof inventory === 'object';
  if (!isValid) {
    errors.push(error('invalid_inventory_root', null, typeof inventory));
  } else {
    recursivelyInspect(inventory, errors);
  }
  if (!isValid || inventory.schema_version !== INVENTORY_SCHEMA) {
    errors.push(error('unsupported_schema_version', null, isValid ? inventory.schema_version : null));
  }
  if (isValid && !Array.isArray(inventory.rows)) errors.push(error('rows_not_array', null, typeof inventory.rows));
  const rows = (isValid && Array.isArray(inventory.rows)) ? inventory.rows : [];
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. When validating expected array fields from upstream schemas or input data, explicitly verify that all expected elements are present (rather than just checking the array length) to prevent silent mismatches from renamed or replaced fields. If the field is present but is not an array, process it as an empty array and trigger a validation failure to ensure the system fails closed.
</details>

